To make a field stateless using Spring Boot annotations, you can use the @Transient annotation. This annotation can be used on a field to mark it as transient, which means that it will not be persisted to the database and will not be included in object state. For example:


 ```
 @Entity
 public class User {


  @Id
  private Long id;


  @Transient
  private String password;


  // Other fields and constructors omitted for brevity
 }
 ```


 In this example, the `password` field is marked as transient, which means that it will not be persisted to the database and will not be included in object state. This can be useful if you want to store a sensitive field like a password in memory only and not persist it to the database.

Yes, Terraform supports a provisioner called "remote-exec" which allows you to execute arbitrary commands on a remote machine after it has been created. This can be useful for performing additional configuration or setup tasks on the machine that cannot be done using the resource definitions alone.

Here's an example of how to use the remote-exec provisioner in a Terraform module:

```
resource "null_resource" "example" {
  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    command = "echo Hello, World!"
  }
}
```

In this example, the remote-exec provisioner is used to execute the command "echo Hello, World!" on the remote machine. The null_resource resource is used as a placeholder for the remote machine, and can be replaced with any other resource type that supports the remote-exec provisioner.

The remote-exec provisioner supports a variety of options, including the ability to specify the remote host, the user to connect as, and the port to use. For more information, you can refer to the Terraform documentation on the remote-exec provisioner.

